{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AZo2e1kEmteAM96NiJrZ1X","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# §4 Experimental Setup\n2\t\n3\t## §4.1 Dataset\n4\t\n5\tWe construct our training corpus from SWE-smith \\cite{yang2025swesmith}, a large-scale dataset of real-world GitHub bug-fix commits. Starting from 5,029 task instances spanning 131 unique (repository, commit) pairs, each task corresponds to a single bug-fix commit. We expand each task by treating every gold file as a separate training instance, yielding 44,173 instances total [Source: m1_extraction registry, data_count after filtering 6,696 clone/checkout failures from the original 50,869].\n6\t\n7\tWe run the StructFuse extraction pipeline (§3.2) on all instances. After filtering for extraction errors, we retain 14,288 usable instances across 123 repositories. Of these, approximately 34% possess CodeQL structural connectivity data (15,133 via CodeQL + 764 via beniget fallback); the remaining 66% lack structural data due to transient filesystem failures during batch extraction. For these instances, the dataflow-aware strategy falls back to embedding-dist
